WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.960
One of the interesting things about the multiverse
is that it raises questions about probabilities

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:09.030
that we still don't really quite know how
to answer.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:15.250
The issue is that everything that is allowed
by the laws of physics, happens in an infinite

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:17.750
multiverse an infinite number of times.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:23.310
So, for example, you might want to compare
the number of births of one-headed cows with

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:26.080
the number of births of two-headed cows.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:30.420
And you'd naturally think there are a lot
more one-headed cows than two-headed cows.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:36.090
But if there's an infinite number of both,
one really needs some way of regulating things

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:43.410
in order to be able to talk about what's the
ratio of two-headed cows to one-headed cows.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:49.970
The current status of things is that we can
devise ways of regularizing these probabilities

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:52.750
of turning the infinities into finite ratios.

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:55.550
But there are different ways of doing it.

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:57.880
There's no unique solution.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:02.650
And it even turns out that some of the simple
things that you might try turn out to give

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:06.300
results that are very crazy sounding.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:13.190
So we think that we have narrowed down a range
of possible prescriptions for defining probabilities.

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:17.630
But we still don't know the answer to what
is it that actually determines what are the

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:21.040
correct probabilities in the context of the
multiverse.

